# Linux (LF) newlines, in every text file, on every machine.
#
# Without this rule the line endings a commit gets depend on the machine and
# the editor: Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config,
# which checks text out CRLF, and under core.autocrlf=false a file an editor
# saved CRLF is committed CRLF as-is.
#
# Declared here, the rule travels with the repository: text is stored LF and
# checked out LF everywhere. Every text blob is ALREADY stored LF, verified
# byte-exactly: `git add --renormalize .` under this rule produces no index
# change, so nothing below rewrites history.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The images -- the README banner and the example textures -- are real
# binaries and must never be touched. `text=auto` already detects them by
# their NUL bytes; naming them makes it certain rather than inferred.

*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
